## Tutorials

From the examples you can guess:

Variables are identified withnamescalledidentifiers.

Names can be short like x, y, z.

Names can be descriptive like age, sum, carName.

The rules for constructing names (identifiers) are:

JavaScript treats underscore as a letter.

Identifiers containing _ are valid variable names:

A convention among professional programmers is to start a name with underscore for "private" variables.

JavaScript also treats a dollar sign as a letter.

Identifiers containing $ are valid variable names:

Using the $ is not very common in JavaScript, but professional programmers often use it as an alias
for the main function in JavaScript libraries.

Creating a variablein JavaScript is calleddeclaringa variable.

You declare a JavaScript variable with theletkeyword or
theconstkeyword.

After the declaration, the variable has no value (technically it isundefined).

Toassigna value to the variable, use the equal sign:

Most often you will assign a value to the variable when you declare it:

Always useconstif the value should not be changed

The two variablesprice1andprice2are declared with theconstkeyword.

The values of price1 and price2 cannot be changed.

The variabletotalis declared with theletkeyword.

The value of total can be changed.

Undeclared variables areautomatically declaredwhen first used:

Thevarkeyword was used in all JavaScript code before 2015.

Theletandconstkeywords were new to JavaScript in 2015.

JavaScript variables can hold8 datatypes, but for now, just think ofnumbersandstrings.

Stringsare text writteninside quotes.

Numbersare writtenwithout quotes.

If you put a number in quotes, it will be treated as a text string.

You can declare many variables in one statement.

Start the statement withletorconstand separate the variables bycomma:

A declaration can span multiple lines:

In JavaScript, the equal sign (=) is anassignmentoperator, not anequal tooperator.

This is different from algebra. The following does not make sense in algebra:

In JavaScript, however, it makes perfect sense: it assigns the value of x + 5 to 
x.

(It calculates the value of x + 5 and puts the result into x. The value of x 
is incremented by 5.)

As with algebra, you can do arithmetic with JavaScript variables, using 
operators like=and+:

You can also add strings, but strings will be concatenated:

